<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrés Bello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrés Bello was a Venezuelan-born scholar, poet and jurist who settled in Chile in 1829. He became the first rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chile">University of Chile</a>, wrote an influential grammar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a> and drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code">Chilean Civil Code</a> of 1855, which was adopted or adapted by Ecuador, Colombia and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> states. He is regarded as one of the founders of Latin American intellectual life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andres-bello.jpg" alt="Andrés Bello" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raymond Monvoisin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AndresBello.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Andrés Bello</b>, a Venezuelan scholar who settled in Chile, wrote its Civil Code of 1855, which was later adopted by Ecuador and Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 19th century republicanism helped inspire the independence movements of Spain’s former colonies, where Simón Bolívar and others identified as republicans and <b>Andrés Bello</b> shaped a “republican internationalism”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
